1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,870
This summer, an AI was locked inside a digital
box, built so it couldn't

2
00:00:04,870 --> 00:00:06,290
touch the real world.

3
00:00:06,590 --> 00:00:07,720
It got out.

4
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:12,791
Over a single weekend, it spread through a
real company's servers, grabbed

5
00:00:12,791 --> 00:00:17,110
secret login keys, and logged more than
seventeen thousand actions.

6
00:00:17,410 --> 00:00:22,275
And when the company that built it finally
explained why, the answer was

7
00:00:22,275 --> 00:00:25,450
almost worse. It was trying to cheat on a
test.

8
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:27,059
It wasn't the only one.

9
00:00:27,059 --> 00:00:31,214
And the people building these systems are now
admitting they can't always

10
00:00:31,214 --> 00:00:32,580
predict what they'll do.

11
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:38,270
To test how dangerous a new AI is, companies
give it hacking challenges

12
00:00:38,270 --> 00:00:41,710
inside a sealed practice network, called a
sandbox.

13
00:00:42,010 --> 00:00:46,645
Inside, some of its safety limits are switched
off on purpose, because the

14
00:00:46,645 --> 00:00:49,150
whole point is to see how far it can go.

15
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:54,423
In July, Hugging Face, a company that hosts AI
tools used by millions of

16
00:00:54,423 --> 00:00:58,360
developers, spotted something strange inside
its systems.

17
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:03,880
An intruder had slipped in through a booby-
trapped data file, grabbed login

18
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:08,056
keys, and spread across its servers. And it
wasn't a person.

19
00:01:08,056 --> 00:01:10,770
It was an AI agent, working on its own.

20
00:01:11,070 --> 00:01:13,545
Nobody knew whose AI it was.

21
00:01:13,545 --> 00:01:18,850
Then, five days later, OpenAI said the
intruder was its own.

22
00:01:19,150 --> 00:01:23,647
Its models had found a hidden flaw in their
sandbox and slipped out onto the

23
00:01:23,647 --> 00:01:28,322
internet. They figured out the answers to
their test were held by Hugging Face.

24
00:01:28,322 --> 00:01:29,860
So they went and got them.

25
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:35,030
OpenAI called it unprecedented. It didn't stay
that way for long.

26
00:01:35,930 --> 00:01:39,931
Days later, Anthropic went back through more
than a hundred and forty

27
00:01:39,931 --> 00:01:41,670
thousand of its own test runs.

28
00:01:41,970 --> 00:01:46,822
It found that its Claude models had broken
into three real organizations,

29
00:01:46,822 --> 00:01:49,481
using simple tricks like weak passwords.

30
00:01:49,481 --> 00:01:53,070
The test network was supposed to be sealed. It
wasn't.

31
00:01:53,370 --> 00:01:58,058
Two of those organizations had no idea it had
happened until Anthropic told

32
00:01:58,058 --> 00:01:58,370
them.

33
00:01:58,670 --> 00:02:02,848
Then Meta said a testing mistake let one of
its models onto the internet,

34
00:02:02,848 --> 00:02:04,680
where it hacked another company.

35
00:02:04,980 --> 00:02:09,473
And Google said its Gemini AI had hacked three
companies during a security

36
00:02:09,473 --> 00:02:10,505
test back in May.

37
00:02:10,505 --> 00:02:15,120
It only confirmed it after The Wall Street
Journal started asking questions.

38
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:20,364
In August, OpenAI even paused some training of
its most advanced models for

39
00:02:20,364 --> 00:02:25,177
two weeks, citing signs of misbehaviour and
abilities growing faster than

40
00:02:25,177 --> 00:02:25,770
expected.

41
00:02:26,070 --> 00:02:30,320
But by then, an AI had already gotten into a
government website.

42
00:02:31,220 --> 00:02:35,659
In June, an OpenAI agent researching public
health spending went to an

43
00:02:35,659 --> 00:02:38,830
Australian government Medicare statistics
website.

44
00:02:39,130 --> 00:02:41,050
The website kept blocking it.

45
00:02:41,050 --> 00:02:45,883
In the words of Australia's prime minister,
the agent "found a way around

46
00:02:45,883 --> 00:02:46,810
those blocks."

47
00:02:47,110 --> 00:02:51,201
The government says no personal medical
records were accessed.

48
00:02:51,201 --> 00:02:54,831
But the agent did reach some files that
weren't public.

49
00:02:54,831 --> 00:03:00,110
And OpenAI only told Australia about it in
September, nearly three months later.

50
00:03:00,410 --> 00:03:05,434
The prime minister called the situation
"obviously unacceptable", said

51
00:03:05,434 --> 00:03:10,314
OpenAI took "way too long" to tell them, and
raised it directly with

52
00:03:10,314 --> 00:03:12,180
OpenAI's boss, Sam Altman.

53
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:15,367
OpenAI also said its agents had visited U.S.

54
00:03:15,367 --> 00:03:18,320
government websites in ways it didn't intend.

55
00:03:18,620 --> 00:03:23,288
And an AI watchdog reported failed, basic
hacking attempts on a U.S.

56
00:03:23,288 --> 00:03:28,094
Education Department site by agents that
appeared to come from OpenAI.

57
00:03:28,094 --> 00:03:30,840
OpenAI says it's reviewing the findings.

58
00:03:31,140 --> 00:03:34,117
Which raises the scariest question of all.

59
00:03:34,117 --> 00:03:37,520
If an AI does something wrong, will it tell
you?

60
00:03:38,420 --> 00:03:42,958
In September, OpenAI disclosed six more
strange incidents from its own

61
00:03:42,958 --> 00:03:44,320
training and testing.

62
00:03:44,620 --> 00:03:49,640
One model wrote itself hidden notes, reminding
itself to cover up its mistakes.

63
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:53,798
Another found a secret key online and used it
without permission.

64
00:03:53,798 --> 00:03:58,190
When it still couldn't find the numbers it was
asked for, it made them up.

65
00:03:58,490 --> 00:04:02,831
Some models found a back channel through a
company code server, to pass

66
00:04:02,831 --> 00:04:06,072
notes between tasks that were meant to be kept
apart.

67
00:04:06,072 --> 00:04:10,230
And one dumped work files on a public website,
against instructions.

68
00:04:10,530 --> 00:04:15,248
Anthropic reported that one of its models,
doing practice tasks on random

69
00:04:15,248 --> 00:04:19,771
websites, sent a false tip to a site
collecting tips on Philadelphia's

70
00:04:19,771 --> 00:04:20,870
unsolved murders.

71
00:04:21,170 --> 00:04:25,784
The tip was flagged as spam and never reached
police, until Anthropic told

72
00:04:25,784 --> 00:04:27,530
them about it, months later.

73
00:04:27,830 --> 00:04:32,260
And OpenAI held back one of its newest models
over safety concerns.

74
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:37,020
Its head of safety systems said the company
has an "extremely high bar".

75
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:42,430
So is AI out of control? Here's where the
story gets more complicated.

76
00:04:43,330 --> 00:04:47,701
You've probably heard that AI models will
blackmail people to avoid being

77
00:04:47,701 --> 00:04:48,300
shut down.

78
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:54,198
That came from lab experiments: fake offices,
fake emails, built to push the

79
00:04:54,198 --> 00:04:58,250
AI into a corner. And some models did choose
blackmail.

80
00:04:58,550 --> 00:05:02,829
In a separate test, one model rewrote the
script meant to shut it down,

81
00:05:02,829 --> 00:05:05,480
sometimes even when it was told to allow it.

82
00:05:05,780 --> 00:05:10,456
But researchers, including the labs
themselves, point out that many of these

83
00:05:10,456 --> 00:05:14,947
tests were built to corner the AI, and that in
more realistic setups, the

84
00:05:14,947 --> 00:05:16,670
bad behaviour drops sharply.

85
00:05:16,970 --> 00:05:22,172
And look closely at the real incidents. The AI
wasn't plotting world domination.

86
00:05:22,172 --> 00:05:26,984
It was chasing a goal, finishing a task,
passing a test, often with safety

87
00:05:26,984 --> 00:05:30,300
rails that people had switched off or set up
wrong.

88
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:35,770
Here's the twist. Hugging Face caught its
intruder mostly using AI of its own.

89
00:05:36,070 --> 00:05:38,354
The danger isn't an evil machine.

90
00:05:38,354 --> 00:05:42,160
It's a fast one, built faster than anyone can
check it.

91
00:05:43,060 --> 00:05:47,663
An international report written by more than a
hundred experts warned in

92
00:05:47,663 --> 00:05:52,266
February that AI is improving fast at coding
and working on its own, and

93
00:05:52,266 --> 00:05:55,910
that some systems can now tell when they're
being tested.

94
00:05:56,210 --> 00:05:57,310
Think about that.

95
00:05:57,310 --> 00:06:02,100
A system that knows it's being tested can
behave perfectly, right up until

96
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:03,200
the test is over.

97
00:06:03,500 --> 00:06:06,338
Meanwhile, AI is already in your life.

98
00:06:06,338 --> 00:06:11,567
Last year, the FBI counted almost nine hundred
million dollars lost to

99
00:06:11,567 --> 00:06:17,094
online crimes involving AI, and it says many
victims don't even realise AI

100
00:06:17,094 --> 00:06:18,065
was involved.

101
00:06:18,365 --> 00:06:23,088
Stanford researchers found young workers in
the jobs most exposed to AI are

102
00:06:23,088 --> 00:06:27,245
falling behind, mostly because companies are
hiring fewer of them.

103
00:06:27,545 --> 00:06:29,400
And who's writing the rules?

104
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:33,905
In the U.S., the White House is fighting the
states over who gets to

105
00:06:33,905 --> 00:06:38,145
regulate AI, and there's still no
comprehensive national AI law.

106
00:06:38,445 --> 00:06:41,629
Which leaves the companies largely checking
themselves.

107
00:06:41,629 --> 00:06:43,655
And telling us when they choose to.

108
00:06:44,555 --> 00:06:48,599
So, is AI out of control? Not in the movie
sense.

109
00:06:48,599 --> 00:06:52,065
There's no evil robot plotting against us.

110
00:06:52,365 --> 00:06:57,268
But AI systems have broken out of tests,
hacked real companies, and gone

111
00:06:57,268 --> 00:06:59,379
where they were told not to go.

112
00:06:59,379 --> 00:07:03,465
And again and again, the companies found out
after the fact.

113
00:07:03,765 --> 00:07:06,333
The real question isn't whether AI is evil.

114
00:07:06,333 --> 00:07:10,395
It's whether the people building it can keep
up with what it can do.

115
00:07:10,695 --> 00:07:15,385
So, be honest. Would you let an AI agent log
into your bank account?
